<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Plaintext traffic (http) policy.

  ── Why it has to be opened up──────────────────────────────────────────
  Android 9(from API 28) plaintext http is forbidden by default. The gateway runs on a LAN and usually has no
  certificate; without opening it up, every request gets blocked by the system outright—— the error is "Cleartext HTTP
  traffic not permitted; in the WebView it only looks like a fetch failure — from the UI
  the reason is not visible at all.

  ── why open it up globally instead of only for the LAN segments──────────────────
  Wanted to open it up per network segment, but Android— see the `<domain>` Only exact hostnames / IPs, or a leading dot, are supported
  s suffix, **Not supported CIDR**.`192.168.0.0/16` This form of writing is ineffective; enumerate the private
  enumerating every IP of a network segment is not realistic either. And the gateway address is user-entered, so it may fall in any private segment
  (10.x / 172.16–31.x / 192.168.x) or tailscale— see the 100.64.x in it.

  So plaintext is opened up globally here, and the risk is recorded honestly here:
    · plaintext only protects the "link", not the "service"—— The panel still requires an admin login;
    · the risk surface is "passwords and tokens can be sniffed within the same LAN";
    · To eliminate it, configure TLS for the gateway and enter the https address in the app (the code takes the same",
      paths,`normalizeBase` only http was filled in because that is the most common form).
  For a tool that "connects to a gateway on its own LAN", this trade-off is more acceptable than "cannot connect".
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
